WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.292
- What's harder to win:

00:00:01.292 --> 00:00:03.712
an NBA Finals or a Super Bowl?

00:00:03.712 --> 00:00:04.546
- Super Bowl.

00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:05.046
- Why?

00:00:05.046 --> 00:00:06.297
- You only get one shot.
- Right.

00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:09.759
- Cuz you could be a better
team 17 weeks out of 17

00:00:09.759 --> 00:00:11.594
and still lose on that
Super Bowl Sunday.

00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:14.264
♪♪♪

00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:25.150
- Welcome to another
episode of "Full-Bodied."

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.945
I'm Channing Frye, here with
Cam Jordan, the pride and joy

00:00:28.945 --> 00:00:30.488
of the Chandler Wolves.

00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:32.907
Thank you for being
on the show with us.

00:00:32.907 --> 00:00:35.577
As the wine gets darker, the
questions gonna get deeper.

00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:36.619
- Hey, what's up?

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:37.370
How is everybody?

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.455
Wonderful.

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:41.499
Yes, Saints may or may not
win the Super Bowl this year.

00:00:41.499 --> 00:00:42.584
Guarantee you that.

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:44.836
- You might be one of the
coolest people I've never met,

00:00:44.836 --> 00:00:46.838
and I feel like I've met
a lotta cool people.

00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:49.966
So, cheers, and we're gonna
get this thing started.

00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:52.594
So this first one is
a sauvignon blanc.

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:56.222
And sav blanc is generally
known as like an old-school wine,

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:59.517
but a lot of people are
doing it differently now.

00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:03.772
And so, my first question
is, how are the Saints

00:01:03.772 --> 00:01:05.690
going to be better?

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.442
How are they gonna
reinvent themselves?

00:01:07.442 --> 00:01:08.943
- You know, as we talk
about this wine,

00:01:08.943 --> 00:01:10.445
it's definitely a
dawn of a new age.

00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:12.238
You know, Drew
Brees just retired.

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.741
We're heading into a
new leadership position

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:16.201
in the quarterback room.

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:18.620
But at the same time,
in the last three years,

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.955
you've seen a
culture shift as well.

00:01:20.955 --> 00:01:23.208
It hasn't just been the
New Orleans Saints offense.

00:01:23.208 --> 00:01:25.085
You now have the
defense that comes too.

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:26.795
And so, when you think
about our defense,

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:29.130
we've got leadership at every position.
- Right, right.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.092
- Where, you know, it's a stalwart,
where we can be dependable.

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:35.011
So I think that our leadership
will come from our defense.

00:01:35.011 --> 00:01:36.471
But we talk about
offense, you still

00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:39.933
have AK, which is probably the
best well-balanced, all-around,

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.976
running back you're gonna
catch in the league.

00:01:41.976 --> 00:01:43.770
- You're making me wanna buy
a Saints jersey,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.271
my friend. Shit.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.941
- I mean, you talk about what
he's able to do on the field,

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.526
whether it's running
in between guards,

00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:51.402
bouncing outside,
catching the rock.

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:52.946
I mean, the moment
he hits open space,

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:54.114
it's a full-blown problem.

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:56.491
I remember he came in his rookie
year and I tried to smack him.
- Yeah.

00:01:56.491 --> 00:01:58.535
- Like, it's this training
camp and I was like, "I'mma

00:01:58.535 --> 00:02:00.411
test what he's worth," you know?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:00.411 --> 00:02:02.789
- And so I went to go smash
him, and I think he like—

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:05.458
I lifted him at least
two feet in the air, you know?
- Right. At least.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:06.251
- Like, he bounced.

00:02:06.251 --> 00:02:08.670
I was like, I was like, "Good
hit. Clean hit. I love it."

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:09.879
I was like, "Welcome."

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:12.924
But he lands, plants
his foot down

00:02:12.924 --> 00:02:14.425
and was, like, ready for the next move.

00:02:14.425 --> 00:02:15.885
And I was like,
"Oh, he's different."

00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:18.096
Luckily, it was out of bounds so he was—

00:02:18.096 --> 00:02:19.931
- The play was over.
- It would have been tipped fair
had it been a game—

00:02:19.931 --> 00:02:22.267
- It's like when you're late
for a rotation and somebody

00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:24.894
goes to dunk and the
ball bounces real far—
- No doubt.

00:02:24.894 --> 00:02:28.565
- You look— like, you see, you see
Jesus say, "You owe me one.

00:02:28.565 --> 00:02:29.899
You owe me one."
- Exactly.

00:02:29.899 --> 00:02:32.944
- Cuz I almost got boomed
on one time, and I was like,

00:02:32.944 --> 00:02:34.946
"I woulda had to quit
playing basketball.

00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:36.781
I woulda had to quit.

00:02:36.781 --> 00:02:40.326
I can't go home. I can't
see my family. I can't see—

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:43.246
I gotta just, I gotta go work at Best Buy."
"Channing, what happened to you?"

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:44.205
"You saw it.

00:02:44.205 --> 00:02:45.373
You saw I got dunked."

00:02:45.373 --> 00:02:48.168
So this enthusiasm for
the game, and you've

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:51.713
been in there for a long
time for any NFL player.

00:02:51.713 --> 00:02:54.966
You're a Pro Bowler. You're
from Chandler, Arizona.

00:02:54.966 --> 00:02:57.051
And funny enough, I'm
from Chandler, Arizona.

00:02:57.051 --> 00:03:00.805
So coming from where we
come from, why football?

00:03:00.805 --> 00:03:03.224
- So Pops played in
the NFL for 13 years.
- I know that.

00:03:03.224 --> 00:03:06.060
- Six-time Pro Bowler, Minnesota
Vikings. From Phoenix—

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.020
- Right.
- Went to South Phoenix.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.814
Eighth grade rolls around,
I'm probably like 5' 8",

00:03:09.814 --> 00:03:12.150
a buck 90-something, maybe 200.
- Woo.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.651
- I might've been 200.
- Dang!

00:03:13.651 --> 00:03:18.740
It's a very solid 200, it's just—
I'm still, I'm dunking, you know?

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:19.532
I'm dunking.

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:20.325
- So you're bouncing
to yourself.

00:03:20.325 --> 00:03:21.659
- That's what I'm saying.
I'm very explosive.

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:22.535
I was a mini Zion,

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.204
you know what I'm saying?

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:27.373
Hey, I considered myself a point before
but it wasn't working out.

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.042
5' 9", I was on
the B-team. Like,

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.376
shit wasn't working out my way.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.545
And then Pops was like, "I
don't wanna be that dad,

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:33.379
but here's your pads.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:33.963
You got practice."

00:03:33.963 --> 00:03:36.507
He just drops me off, throws
the pads at me, and I'm pissed.

00:03:36.507 --> 00:03:37.592
And I'm sitting there at
practice like, "I

00:03:37.592 --> 00:03:39.385
don't want this sh—."
Like, "What am I doing here?"

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:41.179
I was like, "I'm a hooper."

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:43.389
I was like, I'm like,
"Splash, water, that's me."

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:46.476
I'm looking up to guys like Amar'e, you know?
- Right. Right, right, right.

00:03:46.476 --> 00:03:47.852
- Shawn Marion and Steve Nash.

00:03:47.852 --> 00:03:49.562
I'm thinking, like, the
whole Suns roster.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.147
I'm like, "Man, I'm going
to be one of them one day."

00:03:51.147 --> 00:03:52.815
Like, "You're messing
up my dreams here."

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:54.442
- And then, moving on to college,

00:03:54.442 --> 00:03:55.276
why Cal?

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:56.402
- Man.
- Why not Arizona?

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:57.779
Why not Arizona State?

00:03:57.779 --> 00:04:01.532
- Arizona State had, like, that weird
college coach transition.
- Yeah.

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:04.202
Like, one coaching had gone out,
one coach is coming in.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:05.954
- And then, Oregon
State really loved me.

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:07.664
Oregon State had a
lotta love for me,

00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:10.917
but he kept wanting to
make me into a guard.

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:13.753
- Yeah?
- Yeah, he was like, "You could
be a Hall of Famer gold jacket

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:14.879
guard."
- Right.

00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:19.384
- Like, I do way too much athletically
for me to be a fat person.

00:04:19.384 --> 00:04:20.426
- [Laughing]
- And I'm trying—

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:21.636
- No offense to all the guards.

00:04:21.636 --> 00:04:22.428
- All offense.

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:23.137
What do you mean?

00:04:23.137 --> 00:04:26.474
I go out of my way to
make offense to offensive linemen.

00:04:26.474 --> 00:04:28.768
You know like those
sloppy bigs in the league that

00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:30.436
be like, "Oh,
you can't teach size"?

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:33.398
When somebody gets labeled,
"you can't teach size," you know?

00:04:33.398 --> 00:04:35.066
Like, that's what he is.

00:04:35.066 --> 00:04:39.237
He woke up and was
350 pounds, like, not trying.

00:04:39.237 --> 00:04:42.699
- Who is somebody that you
are like, you respect ‘em

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:46.119
but you, like, love just mushing them?

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:48.413
It could be a quarterback,
it could be a running back, it could be a—

00:04:48.413 --> 00:04:49.539
- It's an entire team.

00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:52.750
I love the Atlanta Falcons, like, for
everything that they are.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:55.336
Bruh, they're a phenomenal
team. They have top-tier talent

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:57.797
from quarterback Matt Ryan.
He had an MVP season in, what,

00:04:57.797 --> 00:04:58.798
like, 2016, 2017?

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:01.551
They had Julio Jones
my entire career,

00:05:01.551 --> 00:05:03.052
who just went over to Tennessee.

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:05.763
They just drafted this kid from
University of Florida, Kyle

00:05:05.763 --> 00:05:06.264
Pitts.

00:05:06.264 --> 00:05:07.890
I think he could
be special, but I'mma

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.435
put hands on
all of 'em regardless.

00:05:10.435 --> 00:05:11.728
Like, I expect to win.

00:05:11.728 --> 00:05:13.855
Like, the rivalry between
these two cities,

00:05:13.855 --> 00:05:16.649
New Orleans and
Atlanta, is real.
- Crazy.

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:18.818
- And a lot of my
early career, it was

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:21.404
like, for us to get to the playoffs,
we'd have to beat Atlanta.

00:05:21.404 --> 00:05:23.656
You know, like, we
go into Thanksgiving, "Hey,

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.408
we gotta beat
these Dirty Birds.

00:05:25.408 --> 00:05:27.076
We beat them, we
lock in playoffs."

00:05:27.076 --> 00:05:28.703
And we'd smack the
hell out of 'em, whether it

00:05:28.703 --> 00:05:30.204
be at our house, their house.

00:05:30.204 --> 00:05:32.665
We're eating turkey legs
on the 50-yard line.

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:33.875
We're singing, dancing.

00:05:33.875 --> 00:05:38.212
And one, because there's never
been a defensive player to sack

00:05:38.212 --> 00:05:40.673
one said quarterback
of a team more times

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:42.592
than what me and Matt
Ryan have together.

00:05:42.592 --> 00:05:45.636
We have a special
symbiotic relationship.

00:05:45.636 --> 00:05:48.890
Over 10 seasons, there's
been an amassed 21 sacks.
- Wow!

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:53.061
- Without him, I would
only have 73 sacks, you know?

00:05:53.061 --> 00:05:54.854
I appreciate him for
at least a fourth.

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.565
- So we were just
talking about people

00:05:57.565 --> 00:06:00.610
you love to shmack and lay down
and whisper sweet nothings to,

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.070
and that's obviously Matt Ryan.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:05.031
Who in your opinion are
the top five quarterbacks

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:06.741
right now in today's game?

00:06:06.741 --> 00:06:09.744
- [Whistling] Man, and I'll say, when
you say top five,

00:06:09.744 --> 00:06:11.954
it's like when you get
to that top one tier,

00:06:11.954 --> 00:06:13.247
you can, like, interchange them,

00:06:13.247 --> 00:06:14.874
you know? My dawg
Drew just retired

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:17.168
so I don't have to worry about
putting him in his tier.
- Right, right, right.

00:06:17.168 --> 00:06:21.422
- So then, you got Russ Wilson,
Aaron Rodgers, Matthew Stafford—

00:06:21.422 --> 00:06:26.928
- Ooh!
- Big arm. Pat Mahomes,
and then I'm either tied.

00:06:26.928 --> 00:06:29.680
It's either Tom
Brady or Lamar Jack.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.850
Tom Brady is fighting for
fifth because he's the most accomplished.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:36.020
- Yeah.
- But if you look at, like, stat lines, throws,

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:39.065
whatever it is, you wouldn't
be like, "He's clearly top two."

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:41.651
- He just, he is Chris Paul when
it comes to the game.

00:06:41.651 --> 00:06:43.945
He's gonna nickel
and dime every mistake.

00:06:43.945 --> 00:06:45.488
"Man, you gonna
give me this?

00:06:45.488 --> 00:06:46.614
Boop, OK, five yards."

00:06:46.614 --> 00:06:48.449
- He's so methodical
in what he does.

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:51.828
He's the most accomplished
NFL player probably ever.
- Is he the GOAT?

00:06:51.828 --> 00:06:54.622
- When I think GOAT, I
think, like, Deion Sanders.
- OK, OK.

00:06:54.622 --> 00:06:57.542
- I think athletically,
like, gift from God.

00:06:57.542 --> 00:06:58.584
- Right.
- You know what I'm saying?

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:03.047
Like, one of those Megatrons,
one of the APs, you know, the Bo Jacksons.

00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:05.216
You look at him,
you're like, "Bro, GOAT."
- Yeah.

00:07:05.216 --> 00:07:08.010
- I look at Tom, and I'm like,
most accomplished ever.

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.304
- Right.
- But I just think my athletic GOAT—

00:07:10.304 --> 00:07:12.515
probably cuz I messed
up and said athletic GOAT—

00:07:12.515 --> 00:07:14.892
I don't think quarterbacks
are in that realm of GOAT talk

00:07:14.892 --> 00:07:17.812
because I think they can Ben
Roethlisberger this thing.

00:07:17.812 --> 00:07:21.732
I think, you know, they could be
possible Hall of Famers and—

00:07:21.732 --> 00:07:22.567
- Never work out.

00:07:22.567 --> 00:07:23.985
When I retired
from basketball, I

00:07:23.985 --> 00:07:26.988
realized that wine was my
dream outside of the dream,

00:07:26.988 --> 00:07:28.573
and I also realized
I had to do it

00:07:28.573 --> 00:07:31.576
my way by creating my own wine
label, Chosen Family Wines.

00:07:31.576 --> 00:07:33.244
And in this segment,
we're gonna dive

00:07:33.244 --> 00:07:35.746
into my guest's dreams,
brought to you by Porsche.

00:07:35.746 --> 00:07:38.958
So we got this cab franc.
This is actually delicious,

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:39.834
this Palazzo.

00:07:39.834 --> 00:07:42.378
And I think for me, cab
franc is that usually this

00:07:42.378 --> 00:07:44.297
is something that's mixed
in with other things.

00:07:44.297 --> 00:07:46.424
You're starting your
own podcast, "Big Truss,"

00:07:46.424 --> 00:07:47.258
which I checked out.

00:07:47.258 --> 00:07:48.134
- "Big Truss," Season 2.

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:50.094
- You're into fashion.
You're a Team Jordan guy.

00:07:50.094 --> 00:07:53.973
So, Cam, what is one of the
greatest accomplishments

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:55.308
of your career so far?

00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:57.560
- You know, I feel like
in my life, I've

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:58.728
probably cried three times.

00:07:58.728 --> 00:08:01.272
Once when I was born—
- [Laughing] Get outta here!
- The first time my mama

00:08:01.272 --> 00:08:03.566
gave me an ass whoopin'—
- Come on.
- And the time my wife

00:08:03.566 --> 00:08:04.942
said we were
pregnant with a son.

00:08:04.942 --> 00:08:06.903
And I was like, it
comes full circle.

00:08:06.903 --> 00:08:10.198
My dad had me. I'm blessed
to play in the league.

00:08:10.198 --> 00:08:11.866
It was like, "Yo,
the legacy continues."

00:08:11.866 --> 00:08:13.409
And I'm big on legacy.
- Yeah.

00:08:13.409 --> 00:08:15.244
- I appreciate everything
that I've ever

00:08:15.244 --> 00:08:16.454
learned from both my parents.

00:08:16.454 --> 00:08:19.540
- What is your dream job
after hanging it up?

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:20.541
- Had you asked me that

00:08:20.541 --> 00:08:22.543
my first five years in
the league, my dream job

00:08:22.543 --> 00:08:23.753
would have been a P.E. teacher.

00:08:23.753 --> 00:08:25.671
I was like, "Bro, I'm
gonna be a P.E. teacher."

00:08:25.671 --> 00:08:27.465
- Dude, what do you think I
went to college for?

00:08:27.465 --> 00:08:30.718
- Huh.
- Well, you have to get your
P.E. teacher degree to coach.

00:08:30.718 --> 00:08:31.886
I wanted to coach high school.

00:08:31.886 --> 00:08:33.596
- Whatever I do, I'm
always in the community,

00:08:33.596 --> 00:08:36.098
I'm always trying to impart
some sort of knowledge on kids.

00:08:36.098 --> 00:08:37.642
Earlier this last week,
I was at a football camp.

00:08:37.642 --> 00:08:39.227
But it's like, just
talking to these kids,

00:08:39.227 --> 00:08:41.229
they try and draw so
much knowledge from you,

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:42.855
but they give back
so much energy.

00:08:42.855 --> 00:08:44.565
It's a give and take,
and it's like, bro,

00:08:44.565 --> 00:08:46.567
that's how I swear people
stay youthful forever.

00:08:46.567 --> 00:08:47.735
- But you're into podcasting.

00:08:47.735 --> 00:08:49.153
So you have your podcast.

00:08:49.153 --> 00:08:50.738
- Hey, big "Truss Levels."

00:08:50.738 --> 00:08:51.531
- Big Truss.

00:08:51.531 --> 00:08:52.573
Yeah, yeah, just released.

00:08:52.573 --> 00:08:53.950
- Your episodes are funny.
- Appreciate it.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:58.079
- You're really good, and I
think your guys' chemistry is

00:08:58.079 --> 00:08:59.121
worth people's watch.

00:08:59.121 --> 00:09:00.498
So where did that come from?

00:09:00.498 --> 00:09:02.291
- My boy, we got
drafted together. We

00:09:02.291 --> 00:09:04.252
played in New Orleans
for eight years together.

00:09:04.252 --> 00:09:07.838
I was like, bro, like, between
me and him, we know at least 50 percent

00:09:07.838 --> 00:09:08.839
of the league.
- Yeah.

00:09:08.839 --> 00:09:10.883
- I was like, "We could have
fun calling people in,

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:12.468
whatever it is.
- Yeah, totally.

00:09:12.468 --> 00:09:14.136
- Just talk—It's talking shit
and having fun.

00:09:14.136 --> 00:09:15.096
- Totally.
- Who doesn't like that?

00:09:15.096 --> 00:09:16.305
- It's the best.

00:09:16.305 --> 00:09:19.976
This personality of yours,
were you always like this

00:09:19.976 --> 00:09:21.894
or was there somebody
that, like, you would say

00:09:21.894 --> 00:09:23.437
inspired you to be like this?

00:09:23.437 --> 00:09:25.773
- Nah, it got me a lot of ass
whoopins when I was younger.

00:09:25.773 --> 00:09:27.275
Yeah, I've always been this.

00:09:27.275 --> 00:09:29.485
- Like, for instance, you
send wine to somebody—

00:09:29.485 --> 00:09:31.237
- It was a beautiful
bottle of Jordan cab.

00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:31.737
I mean—

00:09:31.737 --> 00:09:32.655
- Jordan is delicious.

00:09:32.655 --> 00:09:34.031
- I sent him my best vintage too.

00:09:34.031 --> 00:09:35.116
I sent him a 2011.

00:09:35.116 --> 00:09:36.534
I really enjoy that year.

00:09:36.534 --> 00:09:37.451
- That's a great year.

00:09:37.451 --> 00:09:38.494
- It was a phenomenal year.

00:09:38.494 --> 00:09:39.745
- I said that the other day!

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:40.496
- I'm just sayin'.

00:09:40.496 --> 00:09:42.915
- Tell the story of who you
sent the wine to and why.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:45.626
- We had trounced the
Carolina Panthers.

00:09:45.626 --> 00:09:48.087
- Trounced is a wild word.

00:09:48.087 --> 00:09:50.006
- So we, yeah, we beat them
the first time,

00:09:50.006 --> 00:09:51.591
and I think it was a close game.

00:09:51.591 --> 00:09:53.134
We beat 'em the
second time, and I

00:09:53.134 --> 00:09:54.385
think it was a little
bit farther stretched.

00:09:54.385 --> 00:09:56.304
So now, they've got some
salt in their wounds.

00:09:56.304 --> 00:09:58.556
You know, the first game, I think I got a
question about how fashionable

00:09:58.556 --> 00:09:59.056
he was.

00:09:59.056 --> 00:10:02.685
And I was like, "Oh, yeah, you know,
he's got auntie swag on 1,000.

00:10:02.685 --> 00:10:03.811
- [Laughing]
- You know, and it was like—

00:10:03.811 --> 00:10:05.730
- Auntie swag! That was when he was
doing the scarf joint?

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:08.274
- No, I think this was right before
the—whatever, whatever it is.

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:09.150
- Before the scarf joint.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.735
- Yeah, yeah. And he's like, "I peep
at his swag too."

00:10:10.735 --> 00:10:12.570
And it was like, it was like, it
was like, one of these playful,

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.280
disrespectful jabs, Bam, bam.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.114
It was a two-piece.

00:10:15.114 --> 00:10:16.657
And I was like, I was
like "That sorta hurts."

00:10:16.657 --> 00:10:18.242
I think it was the third
game that pissed me off.

00:10:18.242 --> 00:10:20.161
He was, like, warming
up on our side, right?

00:10:20.161 --> 00:10:21.662
He's got these TRX bands going.

00:10:21.662 --> 00:10:22.413
He's throwing.

00:10:22.413 --> 00:10:24.749
And I'm like, "Bro, I've got
nothing but respect for you.

00:10:24.749 --> 00:10:28.544
But you being over
here is messing up my—

00:10:28.544 --> 00:10:29.045
- Juju.

00:10:29.045 --> 00:10:32.089
- Yeah, like my feng shui,
my zen, my happiness.

00:10:32.089 --> 00:10:34.258
And I'm like, "I'mma
let this shit rock, right?"

00:10:34.258 --> 00:10:35.760
I think I get a sack
on him in that game.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.220
And so we beat 'em three times
in a row. Like, that's so tough.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:38.596
Like, that's—

00:10:38.596 --> 00:10:40.139
- So tough, especially in football—
- Yeah.

00:10:40.139 --> 00:10:41.599
- With injuries and
stuff going on.

00:10:41.599 --> 00:10:42.850
- And so I think I made a
statement like, "You know,

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.019
he likes my sauce. I'mma
send him some juice."

00:10:45.019 --> 00:10:48.105
And then so I sent him a
great bottle of cab.

00:10:48.105 --> 00:10:49.940
He's too much of
a baller for me.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.193
When I think one of those
physical specimens, he's it.

00:10:52.193 --> 00:10:52.693
- Yeah.

00:10:52.693 --> 00:10:55.696
Like, my first time I ever hit
Cam Newton, our rookie year,

00:10:55.696 --> 00:10:57.615
I hit him dead in the chest.

00:10:57.615 --> 00:11:01.327
And he eats it, turns and,
like, runs for another 15.

00:11:01.327 --> 00:11:04.246
And I'm like "Oh, shit."
- [Laughing] "You different."

00:11:04.246 --> 00:11:05.373
- "Quarterbacks are different."

00:11:05.373 --> 00:11:05.915
- Yeah, they different.

00:11:05.915 --> 00:11:06.666
- Quarterbacks are different.

00:11:06.666 --> 00:11:07.416
He's different.

00:11:07.416 --> 00:11:07.625
- He's a big dude.

00:11:07.625 --> 00:11:09.543
I'm like, "Yo, it's him,
and there's LeBron James."

00:11:09.543 --> 00:11:11.253
And then I met LeBron and I
was like, "He's a little skinnier

00:11:11.253 --> 00:11:11.879
than I thought."

00:11:11.879 --> 00:11:12.755
He's basketball big.

00:11:12.755 --> 00:11:14.465
- He's 270.

00:11:14.465 --> 00:11:15.508
- He's basketball big.

00:11:15.508 --> 00:11:17.051
On a 6'9" frame.

00:11:17.051 --> 00:11:18.219
- That's huge for a point guard.

00:11:18.219 --> 00:11:19.011
- On a 6'9" frame. Right. Zion is big.

00:11:19.011 --> 00:11:19.595
- When I think—

00:11:19.595 --> 00:11:20.179
- Zion is big.

00:11:20.179 --> 00:11:21.806
- When I think big,
I'm like, "Zion's big."

00:11:21.806 --> 00:11:23.683
Like, 6'7", but 2—

00:11:23.683 --> 00:11:24.183
- 285.

00:11:24.183 --> 00:11:25.476
- Whatever, but looks it.

00:11:25.476 --> 00:11:26.519
You know what I'm saying?
- He's 6'6".

00:11:26.519 --> 00:11:30.231
- Like, you could, huh—
- Oh, yeah. You different.
- And be like, "Oh, you like that." Yeah, yeah.

00:11:30.231 --> 00:11:31.732
- He built.
- I feel like if I did this to LeBron,

00:11:31.732 --> 00:11:34.318
LeBron would shift a little.

00:11:34.318 --> 00:11:36.612
- So what NBA player would—

00:11:36.612 --> 00:11:38.781
other than Zion,
injuries aside right now—

00:11:38.781 --> 00:11:40.741
that you think would be
a good football player?

00:11:40.741 --> 00:11:43.244
- Is this panning all time in my
mind or is this just like right

00:11:43.244 --> 00:11:43.744
now?
- I'll say,

00:11:43.744 --> 00:11:45.121
tet's say all time
and then right now.

00:11:45.121 --> 00:11:45.454
So two players.

00:11:45.454 --> 00:11:47.581
- I'll say Big Baby definitely
could have been a 3-tech.

00:11:47.581 --> 00:11:49.291
- He played, I think
he played football.

00:11:49.291 --> 00:11:50.251
- That's what I'm saying.
I think he played a little bit.

00:11:50.251 --> 00:11:51.168
- He was supposed
to go to, like, LSU.

00:11:51.168 --> 00:11:52.795
- I mean, he had the
size, the wherewithal.

00:11:52.795 --> 00:11:53.629
He could have been a pure 3.

00:11:53.629 --> 00:11:55.923
- He was quick too, shockingly
quick for his size.

00:11:55.923 --> 00:11:56.966
- Yup.
- And physical.

00:11:56.966 --> 00:11:59.510
- I think LeBron would have
been a phenomenal tight end,

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:00.636
would have been—
- Ooh, tight end.

00:12:00.636 --> 00:12:03.097
- I don't know if he'd
ever play wide receiver.

00:12:03.097 --> 00:12:03.973
- Nah, nah, nah.

00:12:03.973 --> 00:12:05.683
- He just, he was too tall. Like,
he'd have no knees.

00:12:05.683 --> 00:12:07.184
You're 6'9"
running down there.

00:12:07.184 --> 00:12:09.311
I'm not—like, no
one in our safety line

00:12:09.311 --> 00:12:11.772
is ever gonna be like, "Oh,
yeah, I'm gonna go up top with him."

00:12:11.772 --> 00:12:12.273
- No.
- You know?

00:12:12.273 --> 00:12:13.524
- Taking them shins off.

00:12:13.524 --> 00:12:14.150
- Absolutely.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:17.945
- We've talked about basically
everybody else except for you.

00:12:17.945 --> 00:12:19.697
And so for me, it's
just kinda like when

00:12:19.697 --> 00:12:20.781
we start talking
about you, we

00:12:20.781 --> 00:12:22.116
gotta bring out the good stuff.

00:12:22.116 --> 00:12:22.992
It's a Nickel &amp; Nickel.

00:12:22.992 --> 00:12:24.577
They're an awesome
place to go visit.

00:12:24.577 --> 00:12:25.911
I love their tasting room.

00:12:25.911 --> 00:12:27.413
Their winemakers are cool too.

00:12:27.413 --> 00:12:29.248
Shout-out to Joe and Phil.

00:12:29.248 --> 00:12:32.084
You have established yourself
not only as a Pro Bowler,

00:12:32.084 --> 00:12:33.961
as a leader—you're different.

00:12:33.961 --> 00:12:35.629
But you're different
in a good way.

00:12:35.629 --> 00:12:38.758
And I think you're leading a new
generation of guys who are not

00:12:38.758 --> 00:12:39.884
hiding behind the helmet.

00:12:39.884 --> 00:12:42.303
- These '90s kids definitely
don't hide behind helmets.

00:12:42.303 --> 00:12:44.638
- You've not missed a game ever.

00:12:44.638 --> 00:12:45.389
- God is good.

00:12:45.389 --> 00:12:46.766
- What's different about you?

00:12:46.766 --> 00:12:48.184
- My mother prayed
for me every day?

00:12:48.184 --> 00:12:50.186
I don't know. You know what I'm sayin'?

00:12:50.186 --> 00:12:50.936
There's some luck.

00:12:50.936 --> 00:12:53.856
There's some blessings
that have been passed down.

00:12:53.856 --> 00:12:55.483
And this idea of attrition.

00:12:55.483 --> 00:12:58.152
I mean, when I
think longevity, I

00:12:58.152 --> 00:13:01.739
think about, you know, when I was younger.
I never really understood.

00:13:01.739 --> 00:13:03.824
Like, Pops retired
when I was, like, four,

00:13:03.824 --> 00:13:05.826
you know? My mom, she
was our first goalie,

00:13:05.826 --> 00:13:07.787
first quarterback,
first whatever.

00:13:07.787 --> 00:13:09.830
You know, she did
it all with us.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:11.707
And you just don't ever
think about stopping.

00:13:11.707 --> 00:13:13.626
It's just having that
motor that doesn't stop.

00:13:13.626 --> 00:13:14.460
I look at my dad.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.796
He still works out
five times a week.

00:13:16.796 --> 00:13:17.838
- Man, you know what I'm sayin'?

00:13:17.838 --> 00:13:19.924
- Oh, your dad. Stop it!

00:13:19.924 --> 00:13:20.633
- He's a legend.

00:13:20.633 --> 00:13:23.844
Like, I'll be like, bro, it took two sons
to equal one dad, you know?

00:13:23.844 --> 00:13:26.430
- Yeah.
- Like, I got the football world,
but my dad also went to Brown.

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:27.264
So he's an engineer.

00:13:27.264 --> 00:13:29.058
So, brother does the
engineering thing.

00:13:29.058 --> 00:13:31.477
But it all comes down to, it's
like, the more you can do.

00:13:31.477 --> 00:13:33.813
Like, whatever you think you're
doing, you can always do more.

00:13:33.813 --> 00:13:34.313
- Yeah.

00:13:34.313 --> 00:13:37.650
- So I sort of live like that
1 percent better every day rule.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:40.653
And in that, you know, being
blessed with health.

00:13:40.653 --> 00:13:44.281
And, like, everybody's had nicks.
- Nagging injuries. Yeah, yeah.
- Nags, whatever it is.

00:13:44.281 --> 00:13:47.827
You know, "Oh, I've got a herniated
disc." Whatever, you know, whatever it is.

00:13:47.827 --> 00:13:48.994
- "Oh, I have a herniated disc?"

00:13:48.994 --> 00:13:51.205
- Yeah, you play 12 games
on it. Whatever that is.

00:13:51.205 --> 00:13:53.541
- I didn't play one game because
my stomach hurt one time.

00:13:53.541 --> 00:13:55.835
- This is the basketball
mentality that we talk about

00:13:55.835 --> 00:13:57.586
in the locker room.

00:13:57.586 --> 00:14:00.756
Like, I've gone to the sideline
and relocated my finger.

00:14:00.756 --> 00:14:01.465
- Same here. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:14:01.465 --> 00:14:02.258
Same here.

00:14:02.258 --> 00:14:02.758
Same here.

00:14:02.758 --> 00:14:04.426
None of these have
not been dislocated.

00:14:04.426 --> 00:14:05.970
- Dislocate 'em, you
pop 'em back in

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:06.846
and you keep on going.

00:14:06.846 --> 00:14:08.472
It's like "Oh, Cam,
but you broke a rib."

00:14:08.472 --> 00:14:09.765
So? It was a rib.
- What?!

00:14:09.765 --> 00:14:11.141
- How many other ribs do we have?

00:14:11.141 --> 00:14:12.309
- See, I don't know about that.

00:14:12.309 --> 00:14:13.185
I never broke a rib.

00:14:13.185 --> 00:14:15.396
Obviously cuz I'm
not tackling other 300-pound

00:14:15.396 --> 00:14:17.022
human beings, but—

00:14:17.022 --> 00:14:20.401
- My conditioning mentally
is, if I'm not on the field,

00:14:20.401 --> 00:14:21.360
who's on the field?

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:21.861
- Oh, yeah.

00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:22.236
- You what I'm saying?

00:14:22.236 --> 00:14:23.737
My condition is,
it's like if I'm

00:14:23.737 --> 00:14:25.739
trying to get my team
to be as close as we

00:14:25.739 --> 00:14:27.366
can to be the
strongest link, there

00:14:27.366 --> 00:14:28.617
can be no weak link.
- Right.

00:14:28.617 --> 00:14:30.494
- And I feel like when
I'm on the field,

00:14:30.494 --> 00:14:31.912
I am our best available option.

00:14:31.912 --> 00:14:34.540
So when it comes down to games,
I've played in almost every one

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:35.040
of 'em.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.625
I didn't start
one my rookie year

00:14:36.625 --> 00:14:39.503
because I wasn't in on
some sub-package, which

00:14:39.503 --> 00:14:42.965
pissed me off, and it inspired me to
be like, "All right, well, shit.

00:14:42.965 --> 00:14:44.592
If I can't be on
this nickel package,

00:14:44.592 --> 00:14:46.385
they're gonna see
me as a defensive end."

00:14:46.385 --> 00:14:48.429
I'mma be a
one through four.

00:14:48.429 --> 00:14:50.014
If there's a fifth
down, I want it.

00:14:50.014 --> 00:14:50.639
And I have to—

00:14:50.639 --> 00:14:52.141
I'll fight coaches
on the sidelines.

00:14:52.141 --> 00:14:53.642
Like, "Hey, Cam,
you're out this rep."

00:14:53.642 --> 00:14:54.643
"You're out this rep!"

00:14:54.643 --> 00:14:55.728
Like, "What do you mean?!

00:14:55.728 --> 00:14:56.687
These are my reps."

00:14:56.687 --> 00:14:58.480
And it's like, so, when
you look back at it,

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:00.733
it's like, "Hey, Cam, you know,
you've played 160 games."

00:15:00.733 --> 00:15:01.650
I'm not even looking
forward to that.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:03.027
I'm looking forward
the next snap.

00:15:03.027 --> 00:15:04.236
You know, I'm always
looking ahead.

00:15:04.236 --> 00:15:05.779
I'm looking towards
the next play,

00:15:05.779 --> 00:15:07.323
what gets me closer
to the season.

00:15:07.323 --> 00:15:09.783
- So we're gonna play a
new game called "NBA or NFL."

00:15:09.783 --> 00:15:12.828
And the first question
is what's harder to win:

00:15:12.828 --> 00:15:15.331
an NBA Finals or a Super Bowl?

00:15:15.331 --> 00:15:16.081
- Super Bowl.

00:15:16.081 --> 00:15:16.582
- Why?

00:15:16.582 --> 00:15:18.876
- You only get one shot, one game.

00:15:18.876 --> 00:15:20.461
And the NBA Finals
are seven games.

00:15:20.461 --> 00:15:22.838
Super Bowl, it's you can
win any given Sunday.

00:15:22.838 --> 00:15:26.300
Cuz you could be a better
team 17 weeks out of 17

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:28.510
and still lose on that
Super Bowl Sunday.

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:31.972
- OK, so I don't
disagree with you.

00:15:31.972 --> 00:15:36.310
But you said how hard is it to
beat the same team three times.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:36.810
- No doubt.

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:40.314
- So think about a team that
is equally as good as you,

00:15:40.314 --> 00:15:42.733
that you played twice
already, that you

00:15:42.733 --> 00:15:45.027
gotta beat four times.

00:15:45.027 --> 00:15:47.488
- If it's a sweep, I'd
say the Finals then.

00:15:47.488 --> 00:15:48.697
- OK. [Laughing]

00:15:48.697 --> 00:15:49.531
- But if it's—

00:15:49.531 --> 00:15:50.950
- Just very evenly matched.

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:52.576
- It's harder to
win a Super Bowl.

00:15:52.576 --> 00:15:53.577
Because it's 2-2.

00:15:53.577 --> 00:15:55.996
If it ends up 3-3, it's
harder to win. Like,

00:15:55.996 --> 00:15:56.747
it all comes down—

00:15:56.747 --> 00:15:57.665
now you guys are really—

00:15:57.665 --> 00:15:58.332
- It just comes down to one game.

00:15:58.332 --> 00:15:59.917
- Yeah, it just comes
down to one game.

00:15:59.917 --> 00:16:01.251
- OK, OK.
- Super Bowl, it is just one game.

00:16:01.251 --> 00:16:03.379
You better have your A-game.
- The A-game.

00:16:03.379 --> 00:16:05.381
- Like, Pat Mahomes out
there balling, but he

00:16:05.381 --> 00:16:07.549
had no offensive linemen.
- Right.

00:16:07.549 --> 00:16:10.094
- That's your only Sunday you had
to show up and win the Super Bowl.

00:16:10.094 --> 00:16:15.391
- Who's more unguardable:
LeBron or Derrick Henry?

00:16:15.391 --> 00:16:17.351
- Ooh.

00:16:17.351 --> 00:16:19.311
Which LeBron are
we talking about?

00:16:19.311 --> 00:16:21.647
- We're talking about 2016 LeBron.

00:16:21.647 --> 00:16:24.400
- 2016, 2017 LeBron is hard to—

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.109
whew, that's tough.

00:16:25.109 --> 00:16:27.444
Derrick Henry, you just gotta
catch him going lateral.

00:16:27.444 --> 00:16:29.530
Cuz if he's coming downhill,
you're not stopping him.

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:31.156
I'll probably say it's
harder to guard LeBron.

00:16:31.156 --> 00:16:31.407
- OK.

00:16:31.407 --> 00:16:33.242
- That's like Giannis
coming down full steam.
- Nah, nah, nah.

00:16:33.242 --> 00:16:35.619
- Like, you're gonna—it's
either a foul or a bucket.

00:16:35.619 --> 00:16:38.914
- Last question,
top to bottom, who

00:16:38.914 --> 00:16:42.626
has better athletes:
football or basketball?

00:16:42.626 --> 00:16:44.128
- Top to bottom.

00:16:44.128 --> 00:16:45.254
Man, that's tough.

00:16:45.254 --> 00:16:48.173
Cuz I was initially gonna
say football, easily said.

00:16:48.173 --> 00:16:51.093
Some of our D-linemen
can easily match anybody

00:16:51.093 --> 00:16:52.803
on a basketball roster.

00:16:52.803 --> 00:16:54.054
But then I thought about it.

00:16:54.054 --> 00:16:57.307
If I say your top 10 versus
my top 10, we'd easily have way more.

00:16:57.307 --> 00:16:58.767
- So you would say—we've had this
conversation before.

00:16:58.767 --> 00:17:01.103
But we had it with all sports.
- Right.

00:17:01.103 --> 00:17:03.897
- We go, who's your top
athlete for football?

00:17:03.897 --> 00:17:07.109
You would say a wide receiver
or a cornerback right?

00:17:07.109 --> 00:17:08.110
- Maybe.

00:17:08.110 --> 00:17:08.652
- Right?

00:17:08.652 --> 00:17:09.820
- I might say D-lineman.

00:17:09.820 --> 00:17:10.904
- OK, a D-lineman.

00:17:10.904 --> 00:17:14.408
Who's the most athletic
D-lineman in the NFL right now?

00:17:14.408 --> 00:17:15.451
- [Whistling]
- Pure athleticism.

00:17:15.451 --> 00:17:19.204
- Either Chase Young
or Myles Garrett.

00:17:19.204 --> 00:17:20.414
- Oh, Myles Garrett's a freak.

00:17:20.414 --> 00:17:21.540
- A freak of nature.

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:22.708
6'6",

00:17:22.708 --> 00:17:23.792
270. 43-inch vertical.

00:17:23.792 --> 00:17:25.711
- He's built like an armadillo.

00:17:25.711 --> 00:17:26.336
- Yeah.
- Yeah, yeah. He's—

00:17:26.336 --> 00:17:27.588
- 4.5 40.

00:17:27.588 --> 00:17:28.422
- OK—
- Freak of nature.

00:17:28.422 --> 00:17:33.552
- So Myles Garrett—
- Yep.
- Right, for you is our—

00:17:33.552 --> 00:17:35.345
- Who's your most athletic player?

00:17:35.345 --> 00:17:36.597
- Wow.

00:17:36.597 --> 00:17:40.893
I would have to say
prime Russell Westbrook.

00:17:40.893 --> 00:17:41.393
- OK. Yeah, absolutely.

00:17:41.393 --> 00:17:42.770
- So those two would be there.

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:46.106
Now who's your least athletic?

00:17:46.106 --> 00:17:46.690
- Oof.
- Yeah.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:48.734
- That's why I was initially
gonna say football,

00:17:48.734 --> 00:17:50.319
but I was like, if
you take all of us,

00:17:50.319 --> 00:17:52.529
like, our offensive linemen
are gonna hold us back.

00:17:52.529 --> 00:17:54.448
- Quarterbacks will
hold you back too.

00:17:54.448 --> 00:17:55.574
Tom Brady is not that fast.

00:17:55.574 --> 00:17:56.784
- No doubt.

00:17:56.784 --> 00:17:58.535
Cuz as we talked
about earlier,

00:17:58.535 --> 00:18:02.581
quarterbacks are not gonna be
that athletic in the old mold.

00:18:02.581 --> 00:18:03.665
Right now—

00:18:03.665 --> 00:18:04.917
- Now it's different.

00:18:04.917 --> 00:18:05.626
It's different.

00:18:05.626 --> 00:18:06.251
- I mean—

00:18:06.251 --> 00:18:08.670
- Quarterbacks are very different.
- Yeah. Dak Prescott

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:11.465
and Lamar, Russell Wilson,
Aaron Rodgers, even.

00:18:11.465 --> 00:18:12.716
I'm more worried about where
the quarterback's gonna be.

00:18:12.716 --> 00:18:14.176
- Aaron Rodgers be moving like that?

00:18:14.176 --> 00:18:16.345
- Oh, he's a phenomenal
finesser in the pocket.

00:18:16.345 --> 00:18:16.970
- Stop it.

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:18.847
- Bro, cuz he has
the arm to do so.

00:18:18.847 --> 00:18:21.809
He'll throw 50 off the back leg,
just chillin'. Like, off a fly-by,

00:18:21.809 --> 00:18:22.810
about to get smacked.

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:24.853
I'm more worried about
where a quarterback is

00:18:24.853 --> 00:18:26.855
than I am an offensive
lineman. Not to take away

00:18:26.855 --> 00:18:30.109
from an offensive
lineman, but I do.

00:18:30.109 --> 00:18:32.236
I just think as a natural
enemy, like, they are

00:18:32.236 --> 00:18:34.655
speed bumps that get in my way.

00:18:34.655 --> 00:18:35.614
- Right.
They are a cobra.

00:18:35.614 --> 00:18:36.573
You are a mongoose.

00:18:36.573 --> 00:18:39.159
- They are like an alligator.

00:18:39.159 --> 00:18:40.410
And I would be like a cheetah.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:41.870
Like, they could
overpower me, sure,

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:42.996
but how can they catch me?

00:18:42.996 --> 00:18:45.916
- If you get pancake-blocked,
do you just hate yourself?

00:18:45.916 --> 00:18:47.668
- No, because everybody gets got.

00:18:47.668 --> 00:18:49.586
But I'mma get
them more than I get got.

00:18:49.586 --> 00:18:51.171
- Let me think of who's
the slowest—oh,

00:18:51.171 --> 00:18:53.340
Jared Dudley, by far.

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:55.300
- Don't—look, look, that's
a hot name for me.

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:55.968
- Big Spudley Dudley!

00:18:55.968 --> 00:18:58.053
That's my guy. I played with him.
- I make fun of him—

00:18:58.053 --> 00:18:59.847
- He's one of the best
teammates of all times.

00:18:59.847 --> 00:19:02.182
- I've gone, I go out of my way
to make fun of Jared Dudley

00:19:02.182 --> 00:19:05.269
because as an athlete, he
is the opposite of what

00:19:05.269 --> 00:19:06.728
I think is athleticism.

00:19:06.728 --> 00:19:08.480
- He has athletic hands. We
used to make this joke.

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:09.898
He has athletic hands.

00:19:09.898 --> 00:19:12.151
This is probably one of
my favorite interviews.

00:19:12.151 --> 00:19:14.361
You're the first NFL player
we've had on the show.

00:19:14.361 --> 00:19:16.113
- As much shit as I talked,
I'm probably the last, but—

00:19:16.113 --> 00:19:18.365
- No! No, we want that.

00:19:18.365 --> 00:19:18.991
We need that.

00:19:18.991 --> 00:19:20.617
I loved introducing
these wines to you,

00:19:20.617 --> 00:19:21.869
having sips with you.

00:19:21.869 --> 00:19:22.828
Good luck this season.

00:19:22.828 --> 00:19:25.038
- I appreciate you
having me, especially

00:19:25.038 --> 00:19:26.290
knowing another Arizona boy.

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:27.666
In fact, a Chandler boy.

00:19:27.666 --> 00:19:28.625
- East Valley guy.

00:19:28.625 --> 00:19:29.209
Come on now.

00:19:29.209 --> 00:19:33.922
- Look, C-Town's finest, always standing up.
- There you go.
- To be in the same space,

00:19:33.922 --> 00:19:37.551
I'm just blessed with the
opportunity to pop up.

00:19:37.551 --> 00:19:38.051
- Awesome.

00:19:38.051 --> 00:19:40.304
- And I'm always here
for a good cab.

00:19:40.304 --> 00:19:41.847
- Cheers.

00:19:41.847 --> 00:19:56.403
♪♪♪

